    "text": "To solve the problem, we will follow these steps:\n\n1. **Define the relationships between \\(A\\), \\(B\\), and \\(C\\):**\n   - \\(A\\) is six less than \\(B\\): \\(A = B - 6\\)\n   - \\(C\\) is three more than \\(B\\): \\(C = B + 3\\)\n\n2. **Use the given arithmetic mean:**\n   - The arithmetic mean of \\(A\\), \\(B\\), and \\(C\\) is 10. Therefore, we can write:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{A + B + C}{3} = 10\n     \\]\n\n3. **Substitute the expressions for \\(A\\) and \\(C\\) in terms of \\(B\\) into the mean equation:**\n   - Substitute \\(A = B - 6\\) and \\(C = B + 3\\) into the mean equation:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{(B - 6) + B + (B + 3)}{3} = 10\n     \\]\n\n4. **Simplify the equation:**\n   - Combine like terms in the numerator:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{B - 6 + B + B + 3}{3} = 10\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     \\frac{3B - 3}{3} = 10\n     \\]\n\n5. **Simplify the fraction:**\n   - Divide each term in the numerator by 3:\n     \\[\n     B - 1 = 10\n     \\]\n\n6. **Solve for \\(B\\):**\n   - Add 1 to both sides of the equation:\n     \\[\n     B = 11\n     \\]\n\n7. **Find the value of \\(C\\):**\n   - Use the expression \\(C = B + 3\\):\n     \\[\n     C = 11 + 3\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     C = 14\n     \\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(C\\) is \\(\\boxed{14}\\).",
